---
import {
  ComputerTerminal01Icon,
  GitCompareIcon,
  McpServerIcon,
  SmartPhone01Icon,
} from '@hugeicons/core-free-icons'
import CodeBlock from '@/components/CodeBlock.astro'
import FeaturePage from '@/components/FeaturePage.astro'

const related = [
  { title: 'Claude Code', text: 'The steps for Claude Code, with the hosted MCP and the skill.', href: '/integrations/claude-code', icon: ComputerTerminal01Icon, link: 'Claude Code setup' },
  { title: 'Claude', text: 'The custom connector for claude.ai, the desktop app and the mobile apps.', href: '/integrations/claude', icon: SmartPhone01Icon, link: 'Claude setup' },
  { title: 'Hosted MCP', text: 'Every tool the server gives Claude, and what the server keeps.', href: '/docs/mcp', icon: McpServerIcon },
  { title: 'SEO Skill vs Claude SEO', text: 'How SEO Skill compares with the Claude SEO plugin on GitHub.', href: '/alternatives/claude-seo', icon: GitCompareIcon, link: 'Comparison' },
]
---

<FeaturePage
  title="Claude SEO skill"
  metaTitle="Claude SEO skill for Claude Code and Claude.ai"
  description="Free Claude SEO skill and MCP server for Claude Code, claude.ai and Claude Desktop, so Claude answers from your own Search Console and keyword data."
  path="/features/claude-seo"
  lead="Add SEO Skill to Claude Code, claude.ai or Claude Desktop, and Claude reads your clicks, queries, index status and keyword data before it answers an SEO question about your site."
  {related}
  relatedHeading="Read more"
>
  <p>
    SEO Skill has three parts, and each Claude app uses a different mix. The
    skill is one instruction file that tells Claude which tool answers which
    SEO question. The hosted MCP server at <code>seoskill.dev/mcp</code>{' '}
    fetches your Search Console, Google Analytics and keyword data after you
    sign in with Google. The <code>seo</code> CLI runs on your own machine
    for the jobs a server cannot do, such as crawling a staging site.
  </p>

  <h2>Which parts of SEO Skill each Claude app uses</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Claude app</th><th>What to add</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Claude Code</td><td>The hosted MCP server and the skill. Add the CLI for site crawls, internal links and Bing.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>claude.ai</td><td>The hosted MCP server, as a custom connector.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Claude Desktop</td><td>The same custom connector, or the local MCP server that <code>seo start</code> adds.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Claude on your phone</td><td>Nothing more. A connector you add on claude.ai shows up in the mobile apps too.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Add the Claude SEO skill to Claude Code in 3 steps</h2>
  <p>
    You need Claude Code and a Google account that can read at least one
    Search Console property. Step 2 needs Node 22 or later (the{' '}
    <a href="/docs/cli">CLI guide</a> lists the exact versions).
  </p>

  <h3>1. Connect the hosted MCP server</h3>
  <p>Run this in a terminal. The user scope adds the server to every project on this machine:</p>
  <CodeBlock value="claude mcp add --transport http --scope user seoskill https://seoskill.dev/mcp" wrap />
  <p>
    Start Claude Code, type <code>/mcp</code>, choose seoskill and sign in
    with Google. Access to Search Console and Google Analytics is read only.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Install the CLI and the skill</h3>
  <CodeBlock value="npm i -g seo && seo start" />
  <p>
    Setup signs you in, offers to add an MCP server to the agents it finds,
    and then offers to install the skill. If you said no to the skill (or
    want to reinstall it later), run:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock value="seo skill install" />
  <p>
    It runs <code>npx skills add iannuttall/seo</code>, which lets you pick
    the agents to add the skill to. Pick Claude Code.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Check that Claude can read your sites</h3>
  <p>Ask Claude:</p>
  <CodeBlock value="Which Search Console sites can you read?" wrap />
  <p>
    Claude lists the Search Console and Google Analytics properties your
    Google account can read, and which research keys you saved. If a site is
    missing, add the Google account that can read it on your{' '}
    <a href="/account">account page</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What the Claude SEO skill tells Claude</h2>
  <p>
    The skill is one Markdown file of about 75 lines,{' '}
    <a href="https://github.com/iannuttall/seo/blob/main/skills/seo/SKILL.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">SKILL.md on GitHub</a>.
    Claude Code reads its description and loads the file when you ask an SEO
    question, so you never have to name it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The file tells Claude to use the MCP tools when they are connected and
    the CLI when they are not. It says to start small (a site, then one
    section, then more rows) so a big site does not flood the conversation.
    It also covers three jobs that need care:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The issues section keeps a list of what the last check found between
      sessions. When you say you fixed one, Claude marks it, and the next
      check says whether it still finds it.
    </li>
    <li>
      For a Google update, Claude compares the update's days with the same
      days before and reports the result as timing, never as the cause of a
      drop.
    </li>
    <li>
      For indexing, Claude runs the same index check every week and reports
      new reasons and pages that moved to "fix".
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Connect SEO Skill to claude.ai and Claude Desktop</h2>
  <p>
    claude.ai and Claude Desktop take the hosted MCP server as a custom
    connector. You add it once, and it follows your Claude account to the
    web, the desktop app and your phone.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>On claude.ai, open Customize, then Connectors. Choose Add, then Add custom connector.</li>
    <li>Name it seoskill and enter <code>https://seoskill.dev/mcp</code>.</li>
    <li>Connect, and sign in with Google when Claude opens the sign-in page.</li>
    <li>In a chat, turn it on from the + menu under Connectors.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Every Claude plan can add custom connectors, and the free plan allows
    one. On Team and Enterprise plans, an owner adds the connector under
    Organization settings first, then each member connects their own Google
    account.
  </p>
  <p>
    You do not need to upload the skill file to claude.ai. The server sends
    Claude its own short guide (start with the report, then ask for one
    section) and offers the full skill to any client that loads skills from
    a server.
  </p>
  <p>
    Claude Desktop has one more option. Run <code>seo start</code> on the
    same computer and setup adds the local MCP server to Claude Desktop. It
    runs on your machine with your CLI sign-in and adds the site crawl and
    internal links, which the hosted server does not have.
  </p>

  <h2>Use the CLI when Claude needs to crawl a site</h2>
  <p>
    Three jobs run only on your machine: the site crawl, internal link
    suggestions and Bing Webmaster Tools (Bing blocks requests from the
    hosted server). In Claude Code, the skill runs them through the CLI for
    you.
  </p>
  <p>The crawl needs no sign-in, so it works on localhost and staging sites too:</p>
  <CodeBlock value="seo crawl --url https://example.com" />
  <p>
    It lists pages that return errors, redirects, missing or duplicate
    titles and descriptions, canonical and robots tags, and broken internal
    links. Ask Claude to "crawl example.com and fix the broken internal
    links" and it runs the crawl, shows you the pages, then edits the
    templates in your repository if you agree.
  </p>

  <h2>A worked example: find out why example.com lost clicks</h2>
  <p>
    Say example.com lost search clicks in October 2026 and you want to know
    why before you touch anything. In Claude Code with SEO Skill connected,
    the session runs like this:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>You ask</th><th>What Claude reads</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>How did example.com do in search last week?</td><td>The report overview: clicks and impressions against the week before, by device, with the top pages and queries.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Which pages lost clicks?</td><td>The content decay section: the page and query pairs that lost the most clicks.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Check example.com/pricing</td><td>The live page, its Google index status from URL Inspection, and its queries.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Did anything change around 7 October 2026?</td><td>The same number of days before and after that date, with the Google updates that fell in the window.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Who ranks for "example pricing" now?</td><td>The top 10 from your DataForSEO or Ahrefs key, each with its Domain Rating.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Claude shows what it read at each step before it suggests a change. If
    the page check finds a noindex tag that shipped with a release, Claude
    can remove it in your code and check the page again once the fix is
    live. The CLI runs the same steps when you want them in a script:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock value={`seo report --site example.com --section content-decay
seo page --url https://example.com/pricing
seo compare --site example.com --date 2026-10-07`} />

  <h2>6 Claude SEO prompts that start from your own data</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Ask</th><th>What Claude reads</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Which queries am I on page two for?</td><td>The striking distance section: <a href="/features/striking-distance-keywords">striking distance keywords</a> just outside the top positions, with the page that ranks for each.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Which titles should I rewrite?</td><td>Queries at positions 1 to 4 with a click-through rate below your site's own curve.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Are two of my pages competing for the same search?</td><td>Queries where more than one of your pages gets impressions, with each page's share.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>What do people ask AI about us?</td><td>Search Console queries that read like AI prompts and follow-ups, with the page Google picked for each.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>What does competitor.example rank for that we don't?</td><td>Ranked keywords from your own DataForSEO or Ahrefs key, beside your Search Console queries.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Does this draft read as AI-written?</td><td>The lines that read as AI-written in the text you give it, each with a fix. No sign-in needed.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>How we picked this page's keywords with the same tools</h2>
  <p>
    We researched this page with SEO Skill in November 2026: keyword ideas
    for "claude seo" and "claude code seo", then keyword metrics and the
    top 10 results, from DataForSEO for the US. Searches are monthly, and KD
    is DataForSEO's keyword difficulty from 0 to 100.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Keyword</th><th>Searches (KD)</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>claude seo</td><td>590 (KD 8)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>claude seo skill</td><td>480 (KD 3)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>claude seo skills</td><td>480 (KD 3)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>claude code seo skill</td><td>110 (KD 5)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>claude code for seo</td><td>110 (KD 8)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>claude seo github</td><td>110 (KD 37)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>claude code seo</td><td>90 (KD 36)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>claude for seo</td><td>70 (KD 6)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    On 11 November 2026, the US top 5 for "claude seo skill" held a skill
    listing on mcpmarket.com (Domain Rating 63), two forum threads, a GitHub
    repository (Domain Rating 97) and context-link.ai (Domain Rating 46),
    with Domain Rating by{' '}
    <a href="https://ahrefs.com/website-authority-checker" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Ahrefs</a>.
    A difficulty of 3 and a page one with forum threads on it is a keyword a
    focused page can win, so "claude seo skill" is the one this page aims at.
  </p>
  <p>
    The searches with "github" in them are people looking for one open
    source repository, and this page does not chase them. Search Console
    agreed with the plan: from 12 August to 7 November 2026, the seoskill.dev
    home page showed 317 times for "claude seo skills" at an average
    position of 57.5, with no clicks. Those searches wanted a page about
    Claude, and the home page is about every agent.
  </p>

  <h2>What Claude SEO with SEO Skill costs</h2>
  <p>
    Every tool Claude calls is free with no usage cap. Keyword research uses
    your own DataForSEO or Ahrefs key, so you pay that provider directly,
    and each DataForSEO result says what it cost (the research for this page
    came to under 20 cents). The only paid part is the{' '}
    <a href="/docs/weekly-site-audit">weekly site audit</a>, $50 a month per
    site, which crawls the site every week and checks Google's index every
    day in the background.
  </p>

  <h2>What Claude can see, and what stays private</h2>
  <p>
    Access is read only, so Claude can read your Search Console and Google
    Analytics data but cannot change a setting in either. The hosted server
    keeps your sign-ins and keys, encrypted, and keeps no Search Console or
    analytics rows. Your questions and the data Claude reads go to Anthropic
    like anything else you type into Claude.
  </p>
</FeaturePage>
